            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Gujarati for beak, then the Gujarati for to peck."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: કાગડો",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "કાગડો — kāgḍo — \"crow\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ગઈકાલે એક કાગડો ઘરમાં આવ્યો. — gaīkāle ek kāgḍo gharmā̃ āvyo — \"Yesterday a crow came into the house.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "કાગડો (kāgḍo) is usually traced to Sanskrit kāka, \"crow\", an imitative word that CDIAL says took various suffixes, with -k- or -g-, in the modern languages. CDIAL lists only Gujarati kāu, \"crow\", there, so the path to kāgḍo is not spelled out (CDIAL 2993)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for everyday life at A2."
